{
  "description": "The UML3 palettes CATIA Magic builds from customization/catia-magic, as tools/cameo-scripts/verifyPalettes.groovy reads them through the DSL service (experiment E17). Each button lists label, the element kind its template owns and the UML3 keyword that element carries, so the check fails if a button stops creating a UML3 element. absentCategories are the General View categories the UML3 palettes remove. The message flow buttons (I-36) copy flows whose ends are UML3Messaging ports, because a flow with undirected ends has no related features. The detail views carry the same palette as their compact counterpart and apply UML3DetailStyleSheet, which shows the compartments a rendered view hides (E19, I-36). 2026-09-17 (before running): 29 buttons became definition-and-usage menus; each entry's fourth element predicts the place in the menu ('focused' = the button the palette shows, 'menu' = the other form), following UML3DiagramKinds defaultForm: definition first on the class, entity-relationship and message schema palettes, usage first on the component and deployment palettes. The dependency menu's focus on 'uses' is predicted too.",
  "absentCategories": [
    "Actions",
    "Other Actions",
    "States",
    "Cases",
    "Requirements/Constraints"
  ],
